<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Federation outbox mail poller — LaunchAgent.

  poga-host: any

  `any` IS A REAL ANSWER HERE, NOT A SHRUG (WI-0420). This unit's header says
  "INSTALL IT ON EVERY MACHINE THAT HOLDS MEMBER REPOS", and that is exactly what
  it means. The renderer refuses a template that declares NO host, so saying `any`
  out loud is what keeps "belongs everywhere" distinguishable from "nobody said".

  This is a TEMPLATE. `deploy/install-mail-poller.sh` fills the __PLACEHOLDER__ tokens
  from the local machine and installs the concrete plist to ~/Library/LaunchAgents
  (never committed — it carries machine-specific absolute paths, P3; `.gitignore`
  already refuses `deploy/*.plist`).

  WHAT IT DOES. Drains `outbox/to-<architect-id>/` by delivering every queued brief this
  machine can actually reach, and leaving the rest for the machine that can. On each
  machine that means the members whose repos it holds. Same program everywhere — the
  poller asks the resolver "can I reach this recipient?", never "which machine am I?".

  INSTALL IT ON EVERY MACHINE THAT HOLDS MEMBER REPOS. This is not a Runner-only agent.
  A machine running it delivers its own reachable share; a machine not running it simply
  leaves its share queued, visibly, in a tracked directory.

  WHY AN AGENT AND NOT A DAEMON. It works inside one user's checkout and one user's file
  ownership, and it may need that user's git credential helper to fetch. A LaunchDaemon
  runs before login in a different security context and would have neither.

  UNLIKE the adopt-runner, this job never spawns `claude` and so has no login-keychain
  constraint — the delivery is a file copy and a fetch. That is also why it can run every
  ten minutes without costing anything.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.federation.mail-poller</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>__PYTHON__</string>
        <string>__FED_ROOT__/curate/mail-poller.py</string>
        <string>--quiet</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>__FED_ROOT__</string>

    <!-- launchd sources no shell profile and hands jobs a minimal PATH (WI-0171), so
         `git` and the credential helper must be named here or the fetch fails in a way
         that looks like a network problem. PYTHONUNBUFFERED keeps the log live.

         THE LIST BELOW IS NOT THE WHOLE PATH, and must not be read as it.
         `render_unit_plist` PREPENDS the rendering machine's `~/.local/bin` when it fills
         the tokens above. A member's contract may resolve its apply verb on PATH by
         design (ADR-0103 D8 shape 2) and that is where the operator installs those verbs:
         a real member's apply verb was there once, this unit could not see it,
         and the release stalled until a human ran the apply. The directory is not spelled
         out here on purpose — `~` is a different place on every machine and a committed
         `/Users/...` is WI-0132's defect, the same one the contract avoids.

         `install-*.sh` renders this same file with a plain `sed` over a fixed token list
         and does NOT add it, so a unit bootstrapped that way carries exactly the list
         below until a cutover re-renders it. -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>PYTHONUNBUFFERED</key>
        <string>1</string>
    </dict>

    <!-- Every ten minutes. Mail is asynchronous by nature — the recipient reads its
         inbox at its next session start — so this interval is about bounding how long a
         brief sits queued, not about latency anyone waits on. It is cheap: a fetch and,
         almost always, nothing to do. -->
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>600</integer>

    <!-- Deliberately false. A poller that fires on every login turns "I rebooted" into a
         delivery event, and the first run after a reboot is the one most likely to find
         a checkout mid-something. The next interval is at most ten minutes away. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>__FED_ROOT__/.session-state/mail-poller.launchd.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>__FED_ROOT__/.session-state/mail-poller.launchd.err.log</string>

    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
